/* Week 7 — Groups & Teams (Ch 7) + Group Decision Making (Ch 11 §11.6–11.7) */
SME.addWeek({
  id: 'w7', num: 7, title: 'Groups, Teams & Group Decisions', dates: 'Oct 19 – Oct 23',
  chapterLine: 'Ch 7 + Ch 11 §11.6–11.7',
  chapters: [
    { c: 'Ch 7', t: 'Groups and Teamwork', s: '§7.1–7.8', note: 'Final-exam material and inside Group Assignment #2; not on the midterm' },
    { c: 'Ch 11', t: 'Decision Making (group half)', s: '§11.6–11.7', note: '§11.1–11.5 (individual decision making) is Week 5' },
    { c: 'Slides', t: 'Week 7 deck (32 slides)', s: 'stages · task types · norms/roles · cohesiveness · loafing · group vs team (slide 20) · team types (slide 21) · groupthink (27) · slide 28 approaches · slide 31' },
    { c: 'Video', t: 'Asch experiment (slide 17) · Adam Grant on groupthink (slide 29)', s: 'conformity · avoiding groupthink' }
  ],
  sources: ['Textbook Ch 7, Ch 11 §11.6–11.7', 'Week 7 deck (32 slides, read before the lecture was given)', 'Lesson pages 1BA3-ch7 and 1BA3-ch11 (Week 7 supplements)'],
  keys: ['Five stages', 'Punctuated equilibrium', 'Task types', 'Process losses', 'Norms & roles', 'Cohesiveness', 'Social loafing', 'Team qualities', 'Self-managed', 'Cross-functional', 'Virtual', 'Groupthink', 'Polarization', 'Crowdsourcing'],

  brief: [
    'A <b>group</b> is <b>two or more people interacting interdependently to achieve a common goal</b>. <b>Formal</b> groups are set up by the organization (work groups, task forces, project teams, committees); <b>informal</b> groups emerge from shared interests. Groups develop in <b>five stages</b> (<b>forming, storming, norming, performing, adjourning</b>), but groups with a <b>deadline</b> follow Gersick’s <b>punctuated equilibrium</b> model: a critical first meeting, Phase 1 inertia, a <b>midpoint transition</b> at about halfway, Phase 2, and a final burst.',
    '<b>Structure</b> shapes behaviour. Bigger groups are less satisfying, and performance depends on the task: <b>additive</b> (sum), <b>disjunctive</b> (best member) or <b>conjunctive</b> (weakest link). <b>Actual = potential − process losses</b>. <b>Norms</b> are collective expectations; <b>roles</b> are packages of norms (<b>ambiguity</b> = unclear, <b>conflict</b> = clear but incompatible, in four forms); <b>status</b> is rank and inhibits upward communication. <b>Cohesiveness</b> is raised by threat/competition, success and tough initiation, lowered by diversity and size, and brings participation, conformity and (if it accepts the organization’s norms) success.',
    '<b>Social loafing</b> is withholding effort in a group, in <b>free rider</b> and <b>sucker</b> forms; counter it by making performance <b>visible</b>, the work <b>interesting</b>, members <b>indispensable</b>, giving <b>feedback</b> and <b>group rewards</b>. A work group is effective by <b>Hackman’s</b> three criteria (acceptable output, members’ needs met, ability to continue). Six qualities help any team (psychological safety, reflexivity, shared mental models, improvisation, collective efficacy, resilience). The lecture adds a sharper <b>group vs team</b> table and four team types.',
    'Three team types: <b>self-managed</b> (task, composition “stable, small, smart”, support), <b>cross-functional</b> (six principles, the Mustang case) and <b>virtual</b> (3 advantages, 4 challenges, 5 lessons; trust is the big issue). Then the second half, from <b>Ch 11 §11.6–11.7</b>: groups are used for <b>quality, acceptance and diffusion of responsibility</b>; their disadvantages are <b>time, conflict, domination and groupthink</b>.',
    '<b>Groupthink</b> (Janis) damages mental efficiency, reality testing and moral judgment; know the <b>eight symptoms</b> and the cures (leader restraint, rewarded dissent, outside experts, <b>devil’s advocate</b>). Slide 28 adds whistle-blowing, idiosyncrasy credits and “disruptors”. Group discussion <b>polarizes</b> (risky or conservative shift, depending on where members start). Finally the <b>contemporary approaches</b>: evidence-based management, crowdsourcing, analytics and big data (volume, velocity, variety).'
  ],
  split: 'If two people share Week 7: <b>SME A: Group structure and dynamics</b> (Ch 7 §7.1–7.4: definition, stages and punctuated equilibrium, size and task types, diversity, norms/roles/status, cohesiveness, social loafing). <b>SME B: Teams and group decisions</b> (§7.5–7.8 plus Ch 11 §11.6–11.7: effective teams, self-managed, cross-functional, virtual, then group decision making, groupthink, polarization, crowdsourcing and analytics). <b>Both</b> should know cohesiveness and conformity, because they connect the halves: cohesive groups induce conformity, and conformity pressure is the engine of groupthink. Social loafing is a motivation problem inside every team type.',
  lens: 'Classic setups. A <b>new manager building a team</b>: name the development stage or model (five stages vs punctuated equilibrium), the structure issues (size, norms, roles, cohesiveness) and the team type, then recommend. A <b>team where someone slacks</b>: name <b>social loafing</b> (free rider, sucker) and apply the five counters. A <b>group that made a terrible decision</b>: name <b>groupthink</b> with specific symptoms and causes (leader promotion, isolation, concern for approval, cohesiveness), then the cures; mention <b>polarization</b> or <b>diffusion of responsibility</b> if the facts fit. A <b>dispersed team with low trust</b>: virtual-team challenges and lessons. Tie structure to decisions: status, cohesion and process losses all feed bad group decisions.',

  sections: [
    /* ─────────────── GROUPS & DEVELOPMENT ─────────────── */
    {
      id: 'groups', title: 'What is a group?', ref: '§7.1 · slide 3',
      lede: 'Chapter 7 opens with Whole Foods: autonomous store teams, peer pressure instead of bureaucracy, a two-thirds team vote on new hires, and gainsharing.',
      items: [
        { t: 'def', term: 'Group', ref: '§7.1', def: 'Two or more people <b>interacting interdependently to achieve a common goal</b>. <b>Interaction</b> tells you who is in the group (it need not be face to face or even verbal; telecommuters count). <b>Interdependence</b> means members rely on each other to some degree. A <b>common goal</b> can be anything from having fun to launching a product.',
          after: 'Groups matter because they exert tremendous influence on our beliefs, values, attitudes and behaviours, and because they give us a context to influence others.' },
        { t: 'cmp', title: 'Formal vs informal groups', ref: '§7.1 · slide 3', head: ['', 'Formal work groups', 'Informal groups'],
          rows: [['Origin', 'Established <b>by the organization</b> to achieve its goals', 'Emerge <b>naturally</b> from members’ common interests'], ['Sanctioned?', 'Yes', 'Seldom'], ['Examples', 'Manager plus subordinates; <b>task forces / project teams</b> (temporary); <b>committees</b> (usually permanent)', 'Friends at lunch; the company softball team (slide 3)'], ['Effect', 'Channel individual effort toward organizational goals', 'Can help or hurt, depending on their norms']] },
        { t: 'note', tone: 'trap', title: '“Temporary” does not mean “informal”', body: 'A task force or project team is temporary but still <b>formal</b>: management set it up. A committee is formal and usually permanent. What makes a group informal is that it <b>emerges naturally</b> and is seldom sanctioned. Ask: who created the group, and why? The slide pairs <i>working on a project together</i> (formal) with <i>the company softball team</i> (informal).' },
        { t: 'note', tone: 'lec', title: 'Lecture: “Why form groups?” (slide 3)', body: 'The slide gives one line: <b>means versus ends, and personal characteristics</b>, with no further explanation. A reasonable reading: people join some groups as a means to an end (get a job done) and others as an end in themselves (the membership is the reward), and personal interests and needs draw people to particular groups. That is an interpretation; use Dr. Kassaye’s own words if you hear them.' }
      ]
    },
    {
      id: 'development', title: 'Group development: five stages & punctuated equilibrium', ref: '§7.1 · slides 4–8',
      items: [
        { t: 'flow', title: 'The five stages of group development', ref: '§7.1 · Exhibit 7.1 · slide 4',
          intro: 'Each stage presents challenges members must master to reach the next, and groups move through at different speeds.',
          steps: [{ h: 'Forming', d: 'members “test the waters”: what are we doing here? what are the others like?' }, { h: 'Storming', d: 'conflict, criticism, sorting out <em>roles and responsibilities</em>' }, { h: 'Norming', d: 'consensus and compromise, norms agreed, cohesiveness grows, information flows' }, { h: 'Performing', d: 'energy goes to the task: achievement, creativity, mutual help' }, { h: 'Adjourning', d: 'groups with a definite lifespan or broken by layoffs disperse; rites and rituals' }],
          mnem: '<b>Form, Storm, Norm, Perform, Adjourn</b>, with the question at each stage: <b>Who are we?</b> → <b>Who’s in charge of what?</b> → <b>What are our rules?</b> → <b>Let’s get it done</b> → <b>Goodbye</b>. Exam stems describe behaviour, not stage names: “members argue over who should lead” is storming, not forming.' },
        { t: 'list', title: 'Practical learnings from the stage model', ref: '§7.1 · slide 6', intro: 'The model is a good tool for monitoring and troubleshooting how groups develop, especially new groups. But:',
          items: ['It applies mainly to <b>new groups that have never met</b>.', '<b>Well-acquainted</b> task forces and committees can <b>short-circuit</b> the stages for a new problem.', 'Some settings are so <b>highly structured</b> that storming and norming are unnecessary even for strangers (airline cockpit crews).'] },
        { t: 'flow', title: 'Punctuated equilibrium model (Gersick)', ref: '§7.1 · Exhibit 7.2 · slide 7', loop: false,
          intro: 'For groups working to a <b>specific deadline</b>: long stretches of stability punctuated by a critical first meeting, a midpoint change and a rush to finish.',
          steps: [{ h: 'First meeting', d: 'critical: its assumptions, approaches and precedents dominate Phase 1' }, { h: 'Phase 1', d: 'little visible progress toward the goal; information gathering and meetings' }, { h: 'Midpoint transition', d: 'at almost exactly the <em>halfway point in time</em>; the group may seek outside advice and change approach' }, { h: 'Phase 2', d: 'the midpoint decisions are played out' }, { h: 'Final meeting', d: 'a burst of activity and concern for how outsiders will evaluate the product' }],
          after: 'Groups with deadlines may also show the five stages, with a new round of storming and norming after the midpoint.' },
        { t: 'list', title: 'Five tips from the punctuated equilibrium model', ref: '§7.1 · slide 8', intro: 'For project teams, advertising groups or class project groups:',
          items: ['<b>Prepare carefully for the first meeting</b>; a coach should stress motivation and excitement.', 'Do <b>not expect radical progress in Phase 1</b> as long as people are working.', '<b>Manage the midpoint transition</b>: evaluate Phase 1 ideas, clarify questions with whoever commissioned the work, expect a fundamental change of approach.', 'Be sure <b>adequate resources</b> are available for the Phase 2 plan.', '<b>Resist deadline changes</b>; they can damage the midpoint transition.'],
          mnem: '<b>Walk the timeline:</b> First meeting (prepare) · Phase 1 (patience) · Midpoint (manage it) · Phase 2 (resources) · Deadline (don’t move it).' },
        { t: 'note', tone: 'trap', title: 'Which model fits the question?', body: 'Social development (getting to know each other, arguing over roles, agreeing on rules) = <b>five stages</b>. A <b>deadline</b>, a first meeting that “set the tone”, months of little progress, or a sudden halfway change of approach = <b>punctuated equilibrium</b>. The midpoint is about <b>time</b> (halfway to the deadline), not how much work is done. Punctuated equilibrium does not replace the five stages for every group.' }
      ]
    },
    {
      id: 'size', title: 'Group size, task type & diversity', ref: '§7.2 · slides 9–11',
      items: [
        { t: 'def', term: 'Group structure', ref: '§7.2', def: 'The characteristics of the stable social organization of a group, the way it is “put together”: <b>size</b>, <b>member diversity</b>, <b>norms</b>, <b>roles</b>, <b>status</b> and <b>cohesiveness</b>. Most work groups, task forces and committees have 3–20 members.' },
        { t: 'list', title: 'Why larger groups are less satisfying (five reasons)', ref: '§7.2', intro: 'Members of larger groups consistently report less satisfaction:',
          items: ['Friendship opportunities rise, but the <b>time and energy</b> to develop them may not.', 'More viewpoints prompt <b>conflict and dissension</b>.', '<b>Less time</b> for each member to speak.', 'Many people are <b>inhibited</b> about participating in large groups.', 'Members <b>identify less</b> with the group’s success.'] },
        { t: 'cmp', title: 'Additive, disjunctive and conjunctive tasks', ref: '§7.2 · slides 9–10', head: ['Task', 'Performance depends on…', 'Textbook example', 'Lecture example', 'Adding members'],
          rows: [['<b>Additive</b>', 'The <b>sum</b> of members’ performance', 'Building a house', 'Tug-of-war; your group assignment; charity fundraising', 'Potential rises'], ['<b>Disjunctive</b>', 'The <b>best</b> member', 'Finding one error in a program', 'A puzzle; brainstorming', 'Potential rises (more chance of a star)'], ['<b>Conjunctive</b>', 'The <b>poorest</b> member (weakest link)', 'An assembly line; team teaching a sequential job', 'Mountain climbing; a relay race', 'Potential and actual <b>fall</b> (more chance of a weak link)']],
          mnem: '<b>“Add it up, OR, AND.”</b> Disjunctive works like a logical OR (one star is enough); conjunctive like AND (all must come through, so the chain is as strong as its weakest link).' },
        { t: 'formula', title: 'Process losses', ref: '§7.2 · slide 10', f: 'Actual performance = Potential performance − Process losses',
          body: '<b>Process losses</b> are performance difficulties from the problems of <b>motivating and coordinating</b> larger groups. For additive and disjunctive tasks, actual performance rises with size up to a point and then falls, while average performance <b>per member</b> falls as size grows (Exhibit 7.3). For purely conjunctive tasks performance should fall as size increases.' },
        { t: 'note', tone: 'trap', title: 'Disjunctive vs conjunctive; process loss is not a task type', body: 'They look alike but mean opposite things: <b>disjunctive = best member decides</b>, <b>conjunctive = poorest member limits</b>. On a textbook question, reason from the definition rather than from examples (the slide calls a relay race conjunctive and brainstorming disjunctive; the text uses house building, error spotting and assembly lines). Process losses explain why actual falls short of potential; they are not a fourth task type.' },
        { t: 'list', title: 'What research says about member diversity', ref: '§7.2 · slide 11', intro: 'Compare a homogeneous group with a heterogeneous one:',
          items: ['Diverse groups have a harder time communicating and becoming cohesive, so they take longer to <b>form, storm and norm</b>.', 'Once developed, diverse and less diverse groups can be <b>equally cohesive and productive</b>.', 'Diverse groups sometimes <b>perform better</b> on creative, cognitive and problem-solving tasks (varied educational background and functional specialty).', '<b>Surface</b> diversity (age, gender, race) has small or fading effects; <b>deep</b> diversity (attitudes toward work) can badly damage cohesiveness.', 'Valuing and managing diversity (Ch 3), for example through diversity training, offsets the initial process losses.'] },
        { t: 'note', tone: 'trap', title: 'Surface vs deep diversity; “diverse groups are always worse” is wrong', body: 'Surface diversity is what you can see or record on a form; deep diversity is about attitudes and approach. The text says diverse groups develop <b>more slowly</b> but can end up equally productive, and do better on creative work.' }
      ]
    },
    {
      id: 'norms-roles', title: 'Norms, roles & status', ref: '§7.2 · slides 12–13',
      items: [
        { t: 'def', term: 'Social norms', ref: '§7.2 · slide 12', def: 'Collective expectations that members of social units have regarding the behaviour of each other: codes of conduct specifying what people ought and ought not to do. Their main function is <b>regularity and predictability</b>. Norms form about behaviours at least marginally important to supporters, and arise from <b>shared attitudes</b>, so there is no such thing as a “personal norm”.',
          after: 'People comply because the norm matches their private attitudes, or because groups have a wide range of rewards and punishments to induce conformity. Google’s Project Aristotle found effective group norms the key to better teamwork.' },
        { t: 'list', title: 'Typical norms', ref: '§7.2 · slide 12', intro: 'The text names three classes; the slide adds a fourth heading.',
          items: ['<b>Dress norms</b>.', '<b>Reward allocation norms</b>: equity, equality, reciprocity, social responsibility.', '<b>Performance norms</b>: official norms favour high performance, but groups set informal ones (restricting output under piece rates); specific, challenging goals raise performance.', '<b>Social interaction norms</b> (lecture slide only: how members should interact; no detail given).'] },
        { t: 'list', title: 'Reward allocation norms', ref: '§7.2', intro: 'Four norms can dictate how pay, promotions and favours are given out:',
          items: ['<b>Equity</b>: reward according to inputs (effort, performance, seniority).', '<b>Equality</b>: reward everyone equally.', '<b>Reciprocity</b>: reward people the way they reward you.', '<b>Social responsibility</b>: reward those who truly need it.'],
          mnem: '<b>Deserve, Same, Swap, Need.</b> Most Western organizations stress equity plus equality. Equity is the Ch 5 idea of inputs and outcomes, not “equal shares”.' },
        { t: 'def', term: 'Roles', ref: '§7.2 · slide 13', def: '<b>Positions in a group that have a set of expected behaviours attached.</b> They are “packages” of norms for particular members. <b>Designated</b> (assigned) roles are formally prescribed to divide labour and responsibility; <b>emergent</b> roles develop naturally to meet members’ social-emotional needs or help with the job (class clown, office gossip, “old pro”, informal leader, scapegoat).' },
        { t: 'def', term: 'Role ambiguity', ref: '§7.2', def: 'A <b>lack of clarity of job goals or methods</b>: confusion about how performance is evaluated, how to achieve it, or the limits of authority and responsibility. It can enter at <b>organizational factors</b>, the <b>role sender</b> or the <b>focal person</b>, and decreases with time in the role.',
          after: 'Consequences: job stress, dissatisfaction, lower commitment and performance, and intention to quit. Fix: clear performance expectations and feedback, especially for new employees.' },
        { t: 'def', term: 'Role conflict (four forms)', ref: '§7.2', def: 'Being faced with <b>incompatible role expectations</b>. The expectations may be crystal clear but mutually exclusive.',
          after: '<b>Intrasender</b>: one sender gives incompatible expectations (“take it easy” plus more urgent reports). <b>Intersender</b>: two or more senders disagree (a first-level manager pressed from above and below). <b>Interrole</b>: several roles you hold clash (work and family). <b>Person–role</b>: the role demands behaviour incompatible with your personality, skills or ethics (many whistle-blowing cases).',
          mnem: '<b>1 sender, 2 senders, 2 roles, you vs the role.</b> The four manager fixes come in the same order: avoid self-contradictory messages · confer with the other role senders · be sensitive to multiple role demands · fit the right person to the role.' },
        { t: 'note', tone: 'trap', title: 'Ambiguity vs conflict; role vs norm vs status', body: '<b>Ambiguity</b> = expectations unclear (“I don’t know what I’m supposed to do”). <b>Conflict</b> = expectations clear but incompatible (“I can’t do both”). Within conflict, <i>intra</i> is within one sender; <i>inter</i> is between senders (intersender) or between roles (interrole). A <b>role</b> is a package of norms for a particular member; a <b>norm</b> usually applies to everyone; <b>status</b> is how highly the group ranks you.' },
        { t: 'def', term: 'Status', ref: '§7.2', def: 'The <b>rank or social position</b> accorded to group members in terms of prominence, prestige and respect. <b>Formal</b> status uses <b>status symbols</b> (titles, pay, schedules, office) based on seniority and, more importantly, assigned role. <b>Informal</b> status can come from job performance but also, unfairly, from gender or race.',
          after: '<b>The communication paradox:</b> people prefer to communicate with equals or higher-ups, but large status gaps inhibit upward communication, so it stalls. Higher-status members talk more and have more influence (jurors, for example). Organizations reduce status barriers by dropping reserved parking and fancy offices; email levels barriers by giving lower-status people direct access.' }
      ]
    },
    {
      id: 'cohesion', title: 'Cohesiveness & social loafing', ref: '§7.3–7.4 · slides 14–18',
      items: [
        { t: 'def', term: 'Group cohesiveness', ref: '§7.3 · slide 14', def: 'The degree to which a group is <b>especially attractive to its members</b>. Because it is attractive, members want to stay in it and describe it favourably. It is a relative property: “cohesive” means more attractive than average.' },
        { t: 'cmp', title: 'Factors influencing cohesiveness', ref: '§7.3 · Exhibit 7.5 · slide 14', head: ['Factor', 'Effect', 'Detail'],
          rows: [['<b>Threat and competition</b>', 'Raises it', 'External threat or honest competition builds cohesion (a united front against a takeover). Extreme threat or a hopelessly unbalanced contest does not.'], ['<b>Success</b>', 'Raises it (failure lowers it)', 'An accomplished goal makes the group more attractive (the winning Fit-Rite store gets more cohesive; the loser plummets).'], ['<b>Member diversity</b>', 'Tends to lower it', 'Diverse groups find cohesion harder, though success can outweigh surface dissimilarity.'], ['<b>Group size</b>', 'Bigger lowers it', 'Harder to agree and coordinate; subgroups form.'], ['<b>Toughness of initiation</b>', 'Raises it', 'Hard-to-join groups are more attractive (elite military units, exclusive clubs, Whole Foods’ rigorous hiring).']],
          mnem: '<b>“Tight Squads Make Strong Teams”</b>: Threat/competition · Success · Member diversity · Size · Toughness of initiation. Threat, success and tough initiation raise cohesion as they increase; diversity and size lower it.' },
        { t: 'list', title: 'Consequences of cohesiveness (three)', ref: '§7.3 · slide 15', intro: 'Whether more cohesion is good depends on the consequences and who is judging:',
          items: ['<b>More participation</b>: lower turnover and absence, more supportive communication.', '<b>More conformity</b>: cohesive groups supply information, rewards and punishment. They first increase communication to a deviant, then, if that fails, cut it off and isolate him or her.', '<b>More success</b>: success breeds cohesiveness and cohesiveness breeds success (a reciprocal link).'],
          after: 'Cohesive groups are especially effective at achieving <i>their own</i> goals. In highly cohesive groups members’ productivity is <b>similar</b> (slide 15), and whether it is <b>high or low</b> depends on whether the group accepts the organization’s productivity norms (Exhibit 7.6). Cohesion pays off most when the task needs interdependence.' },
        { t: 'note', tone: 'trap', title: '“Cohesive” does not mean “productive”', body: 'The safe statement is that cohesive groups are effective at achieving <i>their own</i> goals and make members’ productivity similar. “Increasing cohesiveness always raises productivity” is wrong: a cohesive group that rejects the organization’s norms is especially good at <b>limiting</b> output. Cohesiveness returns in Ch 11: Janis saw it as the root of groupthink.' }
        ,
        { t: 'note', tone: 'info', title: 'Next: social loafing (§7.4, slide 18)', body: 'Cohesion fights loafing in some ways (members care about the group) but the problem is separate: it is a motivation process loss.' },
        { t: 'def', term: 'Social loafing', ref: '§7.4 · slide 18', def: 'The tendency to <b>withhold physical or intellectual effort</b> when performing a group task: people work harder alone than in a group. It is the <b>motivation</b> source of process loss (coordination is the other). It is a social dilemma: slacking may benefit the individual, but if everyone does it, group performance is poor.' },
        { t: 'cmp', title: 'Free rider effect vs sucker effect', ref: '§7.4', head: ['', 'Free rider effect', 'Sucker effect'],
          rows: [['What happens', 'People lower effort to get a <b>free ride</b> at fellow members’ expense', 'People lower effort because they feel <b>others are free riding</b>'], ['Motive', 'To gain', 'To avoid being exploited; to restore equity'], ['Sequence', 'Comes first', 'A reaction to the free rider effect']] },
        { t: 'list', title: 'Five ways to counteract social loafing', ref: '§7.4 · slide 18', intro: 'Counter it by:',
          items: ['Making <b>individual performance more visible</b> (keep groups small; post performance; individual presentations).', 'Making sure the <b>work is interesting</b> (intrinsic motivation).', 'Increasing feelings of <b>indispensability</b> (each member has a unique input).', 'Increasing <b>performance feedback</b> (from boss, peers, customers).', '<b>Rewarding group performance</b> so members monitor and maximize their own and others’ effort.'],
          mnem: '<b>See it, Like it, Need me, Know it, Share it.</b> Distractors: “make the group bigger” or “reward only individuals”.' },
        { t: 'note', tone: 'trap', title: 'Loafing vs process loss; free rider vs sucker', body: 'Process losses are the broader category (coordination and motivation problems of larger groups); social loafing is the <b>motivational</b> part. The free rider slacks to gain; the sucker slacks to avoid being taken advantage of.' }
      ]
    },

    /* ─────────────── TEAMS ─────────────── */
    {
      id: 'teams', title: 'Groups vs teams & effective work teams', ref: '§7.5 · slides 19–21',
      items: [
        { t: 'cmp', title: 'Group vs team (lecture, slide 20)', src: 'lec', ref: 'Week 7 slides 19–20', intro: 'A group becomes a <b>team</b> when there is strong shared commitment and <b>synergy</b> so the whole exceeds the sum of the parts (“2 + 2 = 5”). The textbook uses the two terms <b>interchangeably</b>.',
          head: ['Aspect', 'Group', 'Team'],
          rows: [['Goal', 'May not have a common goal', 'Shared, unified goal'], ['Interdependence', 'Low; may work independently', 'High'], ['Roles', 'Less structured or undefined', 'Clearly defined'], ['Accountability', 'Individual', 'Individual and collective'], ['Collaboration', 'Limited', 'High'], ['Success', 'Individual performance', 'Team performance']],
          more: 'Tension to note: the textbook’s definition of <i>any</i> group already includes interdependence and a common goal, while slide 20 says a group “may not have a common goal”. For a textbook MC question use the textbook; for an assignment question that asks you to distinguish them, use the six aspects and mention synergy.', moreLabel: 'Textbook vs slide tension' },
        { t: 'list', title: 'Four types of teams (lecture, slide 21)', src: 'lec', ref: 'Week 7 slide 21', items: ['<b>Process-improvement</b> teams: improve the efficiency of producing a product or service (on the slide but <b>not in the textbook chapter</b>).', '<b>Self-managed</b> teams: full, reduced or no supervision.', '<b>Cross-functional</b> teams: people from, for example, HR, marketing and accounting.', '<b>Virtual</b> teams: for example, a team spanning the globe.'] },
        { t: 'list', title: 'Hackman’s three criteria of an effective work group', ref: '§7.5', intro: 'A good model is a sports team (small, skilled, coached not supervised). Hackman: a work group is effective when:',
          items: ['Its <b>output is acceptable</b> to management and users elsewhere in the organization.', 'Members’ <b>needs are satisfied</b>, not frustrated, by the group.', 'The experience <b>enables members to continue working together</b>.'],
          after: 'Effectiveness comes from high <b>effort</b>, great <b>knowledge and skill</b> and sensible <b>strategies</b> (Exhibit 7.7).',
          mnem: '<b>Good output, happy members, still a team.</b> A team that hits its target but burns out its members fails two of three tests.' },
        { t: 'list', title: 'Six qualities of effective work teams', ref: '§7.5', intro: 'They both contribute to and result from team cohesion:',
          items: ['<b>Psychological safety</b>: shared belief it is safe to take social risks (question the status quo, suggest ideas, disagree); promotes trust and learning from mistakes.', '<b>Team reflexivity</b>: deliberately discussing team processes and goals and adapting behaviour.', '<b>Shared mental models</b>: members share similar information about how to interact and what the task is.', '<b>Capacity to improvise</b>: flexibility when things do not go as planned.', '<b>Collective efficacy</b>: shared belief the team can perform a task (the team version of self-efficacy).', '<b>Team resilience</b>: the capacity to bounce back from setbacks.'],
          mnem: '<b>Safe, Reflect, Share, Improvise → Believe → Bounce back.</b> The first four are foundations for collective efficacy; all five are resources for resilience.' },
        { t: 'note', tone: 'trap', title: 'Reflexivity vs shared mental models; efficacy', body: '<b>Reflexivity</b> = deliberately discussing and adapting how the team works. <b>Shared mental models</b> = having the same picture of the task and how to interact. <b>Collective efficacy</b> is a shared team belief, not the average of members’ self-efficacy (five skilled musicians do not necessarily make a good band).' }
      ]
    },
    {
      id: 'team-types', title: 'Self-managed, cross-functional & virtual teams', ref: '§7.6–7.8 · slides 22–24',
      items: [
        { t: 'def', term: 'Self-managed work teams', ref: '§7.6 · slide 22', def: 'Work groups that have the opportunity to do <b>challenging work under reduced supervision</b> (also autonomous, semi-autonomous, self-directed). The group regulates much of its members’ behaviour. Success depends on <b>task</b>, <b>composition</b> and <b>support</b>.' },
        { t: 'groups', title: 'What a self-managed team needs (Exhibit 7.7)', ref: '§7.6 · slide 22',
          groups: [{ name: 'Task (3)', items: ['<b>Challenge</b> (enriched job: significant, whole, varied)', '<b>Complexity</b>', 'High <b>interdependence</b>'] }, { name: 'Composition (4)', items: ['<b>Stable</b> membership', '<b>Small</b> (Amazon’s two-pizza team)', '<b>Expertise</b> (task plus social skills)', '<b>Diverse</b> enough, but not too diverse'] }, { name: 'Support (3)', items: ['<b>Training</b> (technical, social, language, business)', '<b>Rewards</b> mostly for team accomplishment, with some individual feedback', '<b>Management</b> that coaches rather than supervises'] }],
          after: 'Giving boring work a fancy name (the “Olive Squad”) will not work: it is “a prime recipe for social loafing”. Teams may pick their own members, but need HR oversight and clear criteria to avoid irrelevant exclusion.',
          mnem: '<b>Task, Team, Toolkit (3 · 4 · 3).</b> The text’s own phrase for composition is <b>“stable, small and smart”</b>.' },
        { t: 'def', term: 'Cross-functional teams', ref: '§7.7 · slide 23', def: 'Work groups that bring together people with <b>different functional specialties</b> to better <b>invent, design or deliver</b> a product or service. Goals: <b>innovation, speed and quality</b> from early coordination (versus the old sequential stylists → engineering → manufacturing hand-offs, with “not invented here” resistance). Cross-training is not feasible, which is why the specialists are needed.' },
        { t: 'list', title: 'Six principles for effective cross-functional teams (Mustang)', ref: '§7.7 · slide 23', intro: 'Illustrated by a past redesign of the Ford Mustang:',
          items: ['<b>Composition</b>: all relevant specialties present (labour reps, suppliers, clients).', '<b>Superordinate goals</b>: attractive outcomes achievable <i>only by collaboration</i>, overriding conflicting functional goals (keep the Mustang name alive).', '<b>Physical proximity</b>: close together for informal contact (a former furniture warehouse).', '<b>Autonomy</b>: from the larger organization, and authority to commit their function.', '<b>Rules and procedures</b>: basic decision rules to prevent anarchy (a manufacturing veto on radical body changes).', '<b>Leadership</b>: strong people skills as well as task expertise.'],
          mnem: '<b>“Cool Sports cars Purr And Roar Loudly”</b>: Composition · Superordinate goals · Proximity · Autonomy · Rules · Leadership.',
          after: 'Shared mental models are hard here: told to design a “tough” truck, a designer hears powerful-looking and an engineer hears durable.' },
        { t: 'def', term: 'Virtual teams', ref: '§7.8 · slide 24', def: 'Work groups that use <b>technology</b> to communicate and collaborate across <b>time, space and organizational boundaries</b>. Their primary feature is a <b>lack of face-to-face contact</b> through geographic dispersion, and they are often cross-functional. Tools may be asynchronous (email) or synchronous (phone, chat, conferencing).' },
        { t: 'groups', title: 'Virtual teams: 3 advantages · 4 challenges · lessons', ref: '§7.8 · slide 24',
          groups: [{ name: 'Advantages (3)', items: ['<b>Around-the-clock</b> work (“follow the sun”)', '<b>Reduced travel</b> time and cost', '<b>Larger talent pool</b>'] }, { name: 'Challenges (4)', items: ['<b>Trust</b> (harder to build, more important)', '<b>Miscommunication</b> (lost non-verbal cues and richness)', '<b>Isolation</b>', '<b>Management issues</b> (assessing performance, fairness)'] }, { name: 'Lessons (text 5; slide 4)', items: ['<b>Recruitment</b>', '<b>Training</b>', '<b>Personalization</b> (informal contact)', '<b>Leadership</b> (goals, communication standards, feedback)', '<b>Peer feedback</b> (text only)'] }],
          after: 'Virtual teams share less information but more <i>unique</i> information, and need high volumes of open communication. Orange’s three trust principles: small teams (under 10), strong leadership, a common working framework.',
          mnem: 'Advantages: <b>Clock, Cost, Crowd.</b> Challenges: <b>“The Missing In-person Meeting”</b> = Trust, Miscommunication, Isolation, Management. Lessons: <b>“Remote Teams Prefer Lively Peers”</b> = Recruitment, Training, Personalization, Leadership, Peer feedback.' },
        { t: 'note', tone: 'trap', title: 'The three team types overlap; slide 24 has only four lessons', body: 'Self-managed is about <b>supervision</b>, cross-functional about <b>membership</b>, virtual about <b>location and technology</b>. One team can be all three. Self-managed teams favour cross-training and broken-down roles; cross-functional teams exist because cross-training is <i>not</i> feasible. Slide 24 lists four lessons; the textbook adds <b>peer feedback</b> as the fifth.' }
      ]
    },

    /* ─────────────── GROUP DECISIONS ─────────────── */
    {
      id: 'group-dm', title: 'Group decision making: pros and cons', ref: '§11.6 · slides 25–26',
      lede: 'Final-exam only (not the midterm) and inside Group Assignment #2. Slides 25–26 match the textbook exactly.',
      items: [
        { t: 'list', title: 'Three reasons to use groups to decide', ref: '§11.6 · slide 25', items: ['<b>Decision quality</b>: more vigilance, more ideas, better evaluation (checks and balances).', '<b>Decision acceptance and commitment</b>: people understand and commit to decisions they took part in (participative leadership, Ch 9).', '<b>Diffusion of responsibility</b> (the less admirable reason): members share the burden of negative consequences so no one person is punished.'],
          mnem: 'Quality · Acceptance · Diffusion (the last is a poor reason).' },
        { t: 'list', title: 'When do groups decide better than individuals?', ref: '§11.6', intro: 'On balance groups produce more and better solutions and outperform the average member, especially when:',
          items: ['Members <b>differ in relevant skills</b>, but not so much that conflict results.', 'Some <b>division of labour</b> can occur.', '<b>Memory for facts</b> is important.', 'Individual judgments can be <b>weighted by expertise</b>.'],
          mnem: '<b>“Some Dogs Mostly Wag.”</b> Simple averaging is inappropriate when expertise differs.' },
        { t: 'list', title: 'Four disadvantages of group decision making', ref: '§11.6 · slide 26', items: ['<b>Time</b>: groups are slow because of process losses (Ch 7); avoid them when speed is the prime factor.', '<b>Conflict</b>: personal axes to grind and politics; groups decide better when members feel psychologically safe.', '<b>Domination</b> by one person or a small coalition.', '<b>Groupthink</b>.'],
          mnem: '<b>“Teams Cause Delays, Groupthink”</b>: Time · Conflict · Domination · Groupthink.' },
        { t: 'note', tone: 'trap', title: 'Diffusion of responsibility is a reason <i>for</i> groups, not a disadvantage', body: 'It appears on the “why use groups” list, not the disadvantages list. Members often “abandon ship” afterward with biased hindsight (“I knew all along the bid was too high”).' }
      ]
    },
    {
      id: 'groupthink', title: 'Groupthink & group polarization', ref: '§11.6 · slides 27–30',
      items: [
        { t: 'def', term: 'Groupthink', ref: '§11.6 · slide 27', def: 'The capacity for <b>group pressure to damage the mental efficiency, reality testing and moral judgment</b> of decision-making groups, so that <b>unanimity is stressed over decision quality</b>. Developed by psychologist <b>Irving Janis</b>.',
          after: 'Illustration: NASA’s Hubble Space Telescope mirror, with a dominant leader who isolated the team, ignored three sets of danger signals and dismissed an outside firm.' },
        { t: 'list', title: 'Causes of groupthink', ref: '§11.6 · slide 27', intro: 'Janis saw <b>high cohesiveness</b> as the root. The text says other factors now look more important:',
          items: ['Strong <b>identification</b> with the group.', 'Concern for the group’s <b>approval</b>.', '<b>Isolation</b> from outside sources of information.', 'The strongest cause: promotion of a particular decision by the <b>group leader</b>.'],
          after: 'The slide leads with “too much cohesiveness, concern for approval and isolation of the group”.' },
        { t: 'list', title: 'Janis’s eight symptoms of groupthink', ref: '§11.6', items: ['<b>Illusion of invulnerability</b>: overconfidence, ignoring danger signals.', '<b>Rationalization</b>: warnings explained away.', '<b>Illusion of morality</b>: the group’s decisions seen as morally correct.', '<b>Stereotypes of outsiders</b>.', '<b>Pressure for conformity</b>.', '<b>Self-censorship</b>: members talk themselves out of dissent.', '<b>Illusion of unanimity</b>: everyone seems to agree.', '<b>Mindguards</b>: members who shield the group from contrary information.'],
          mnem: '<b>“In Review Meetings, Strangers Pressure Silent Unanimous Members.”</b> The first four are how the group sees itself and outsiders; the last four are how members behave toward each other.' },
        { t: 'list', title: 'Preventing groupthink and managing controversy', ref: '§11.6', items: ['Leaders avoid <b>undue pressure</b> for an outcome and focus on good <b>process</b>.', 'Establish <b>norms that reward responsible dissent</b>.', 'Bring in <b>outside experts</b> to challenge the group’s views.', 'Appoint a <b>devil’s advocate</b>: someone to identify and challenge weaknesses in a plan; it works only if presented objectively and unemotionally.'],
          after: 'Full-blown conflict is bad, but a <b>total lack of controversy</b> is equally damaging: it contributes to groupthink and escalation of commitment.',
          mnem: '<b>“Lead, Don’t Over-Dominate”</b>: leader restraint · dissent rewarded · outside experts · devil’s advocate.' },
        { t: 'cmp', title: 'Slide 28: approaches to improving decision making', src: 'lec', ref: 'Week 7 slide 28', head: ['Approach', 'What it is', 'Source'],
          rows: [['<b>Devil’s advocate</b>', 'Appointed to challenge a plan’s weaknesses; objective, unemotional', 'Textbook §11.6'], ['<b>Whistle-blowing</b>', 'A current or former member discloses illegitimate practices to someone who may be able to act; last line of defence', 'Textbook Ch 12'], ['<b>Encouraging outliers / idiosyncrasy credits</b>', 'Hollander: members earn credits by conforming and contributing, then “spend” them to deviate without being sanctioned', 'Not in the textbook'], ['<b>Disruptors</b>', 'The slide gives only the word; most plausibly people brought in to unsettle settled assumptions', 'Slide only (undefined)']],
          mnem: '<b>Devil, Whistle, Outlier, Disruptor.</b> Dissent assigned inside · reported outside · earned inside · brought in.' },
        { t: 'note', tone: 'trap', title: 'Is cohesiveness the cause of groupthink?', body: 'Slide 27 leads with cohesiveness (Janis’s original view). The text says other factors now matter more and that the <b>leader’s promotion of a decision</b> is the strongest. For a textbook MC question on the strongest cause, answer the <b>leader</b>. For an assignment, state Janis’s view and then the update. Do not confuse groupthink (conformity failure) with risky shift (polarization) or diffusion of responsibility (sharing blame).' }
        ,
        { t: 'def', term: 'Risky shift and conservative shift', ref: '§11.6 · slide 30', def: 'Stoner’s <b>risky shift</b>: after discussion, groups advised riskier courses than the average of their members’ initial positions. Later work found <b>conservative shifts</b> too. Which occurs depends on the members’ <b>initial position</b>: those who start somewhat conservative move more conservative; those who start somewhat risky move more risky. Discussion <b>polarizes</b> (exaggerates) the initial leaning (Exhibit 11.4).',
          after: 'Example: boards deciding what to pay for an acquisition target; high individual views give an even higher average offer after deliberation.' },
        { t: 'list', title: 'Two reasons for polarization', ref: '§11.6', items: ['Discussion generates <b>new arguments that favour the initial leaning</b>.', 'Members <b>one-up each other</b>: they present themselves as similar to others but “even better”, taking a slightly more extreme stance.'],
          after: 'If polarization comes from a sensible exchange of information it may improve the decision; if from one-upmanship it can lower quality.' },
        { t: 'note', tone: 'trap', title: 'Groupthink vs shift vs diffusion', body: '<b>Groupthink</b> = conformity failure (pressure, self-censorship, false unanimity). <b>Risky/conservative shift</b> = discussion exaggerates where members already stood; the group is not always riskier. <b>Diffusion of responsibility</b> = sharing blame for a bad outcome. Also remember groups are <b>more</b> prone than individuals to escalation of commitment.' }
      ]
    },
    {
      id: 'improve', title: 'Contemporary approaches to improving decisions', ref: '§11.7 · slide 31',
      lede: 'Good decisions improve when decision makers follow the rational model. Slide 31 lists three items (evidence-based management, crowdsourcing, analytics and big data); the textbook checklist counts analytics and big data separately, giving four.',
      items: [
        { t: 'def', term: 'Evidence-based management', ref: '§11.7', def: '“Making decisions through the <b>conscientious, explicit, and judicious use of the best available evidence from multiple sources</b>.” Wishful thinking and intuition are no substitute. It borrows from evidence-based medicine.',
          after: 'Poor “evidence”: guessing what the boss favours, copying competitors (“benchmarking”), canned consulting packages. Ask: <b>What precisely is the evidence? How good is it? Can we get better?</b>' },
        { t: 'list', title: 'Sources of good evidence', ref: '§11.7', items: ['<b>Scientific, peer-reviewed research</b> (the gold standard; systematic reviews and meta-analysis).', '<b>Data from the organization</b> (surveys, financial and HR data).', '<b>New data sources</b>: crowdsourcing and mining archived internet traffic.', '<b>Expert and professional judgment</b>.'] },
        { t: 'def', term: 'Crowdsourcing', ref: '§11.7', def: '<b>“Outsourcing” aspects of a decision process to a large collection of people</b> (usually outside the organization), to develop alternatives, choose the best solution, or both. It is <b>not</b> contracting with consultants; it is open and organic (Wikipedia, Doritos’ ads, the Goldcorp Challenge, LEGO Ideas).',
          after: 'Two ideas: the information is <b>not available in house</b>, and a large diverse crowd probably contains a <b>single superior solution</b> (technical problems) or, on average, the <b>correct answer</b> (style and taste). Works best when in-house expertise is lacking, the problem is clearly stated, and the crowd is knowledgeable and motivated. Firms must build trust that ideas will not be taken without compensation.' },
        { t: 'cmp', title: 'Analytics vs big data', ref: '§11.7', head: ['', 'Analytics', 'Big data'],
          rows: [['What it is', 'Finding <b>meaningful patterns</b> in large datasets with statistics, modelling and visualization', '<b>Copious information</b>, often real time, from varied (mostly digital) sources'], ['Role', 'The <b>method</b>', 'The <b>raw material</b>'], ['Examples', 'Moneyball; Netflix’s recommendation algorithm', 'Social media, GPS, shopping records, sensor data'], ['Watch out for', 'Hostility from entrenched fields', 'Privacy, unintended consequences, overfitting']],
          mnem: 'Big data’s <b>three Vs</b>: Volume · Velocity · Variety. The four approaches: <b>“Evidence Counts, Analytics Back it up”</b> (ECAB).' },
        { t: 'note', tone: 'trap', title: 'Crowdsourcing is not consulting; three or four approaches', body: 'Crowdsourcing is an open call to a large crowd, not a formal relationship with advisors. Analytics is the method; big data is the material. Evidence-based management is about <b>quality of evidence</b> (peer-reviewed research is the gold standard), not just having numbers. The textbook calls crowdsourcing, analytics and big data facets of evidence-based management. Nominal group technique and brainstorming are <b>not</b> in the 12th-edition chapter or the slides.' }
      ]
    }
  ],

  quiz: [
    { q: 'Which of the following is a <b>formal</b> group?', o: ['A lunchtime circle of friends from different departments', 'A temporary task force set up by management to suggest productivity improvements', 'The company softball team that organized itself', 'Employees who share a love of cycling and commute together'], a: 1, why: 'Formal groups are <b>established by the organization</b>, and temporary ones such as task forces are still formal. Informal groups emerge naturally from common interests.' },
    { q: 'Members of a new project team argue over who should lead the data analysis and who should write the report. Which stage of group development is this?', o: ['Forming', 'Norming', 'Storming', 'Performing'], a: 2, why: '<b>Storming</b>: conflict and sorting out roles and responsibilities. Forming is testing the waters; norming is agreeing on rules.' },
    { q: 'A team has ten weeks to deliver a proposal. Weeks 1–5 show little visible progress, and in week 5 it scraps its plan after a client meeting. Which model best explains this?', o: ['The five-stage model', 'Punctuated equilibrium', 'Social loafing', 'Role conflict'], a: 1, why: 'A <b>deadline</b>, a Phase 1 of little progress and a change of approach at the <b>halfway point</b> are Gersick’s punctuated equilibrium model.' },
    { q: 'Performance of a group is limited by the performance of its poorest member. This is a ___ task, and adding members ___ potential performance.', o: ['conjunctive; lowers', 'disjunctive; lowers', 'additive; raises', 'conjunctive; raises'], a: 0, why: '<b>Conjunctive</b> (weakest link, logical AND): more members means a greater chance of a weak link. Disjunctive depends on the <b>best</b> member.' },
    { q: 'A manager tells an employee to “take it easy” and in the same hour hands over a stack of urgent reports. This is:', o: ['Role ambiguity', 'Intersender role conflict', 'Intrasender role conflict', 'Person–role conflict'], a: 2, why: 'One sender gives incompatible expectations: <b>intrasender</b> role conflict. Fix: avoid self-contradictory messages.' },
    { q: 'An employee is asked to backdate invoices, which she believes is unethical. This is most likely:', o: ['Interrole conflict', 'Role ambiguity', 'Intersender conflict', 'Person–role conflict'], a: 3, why: 'The role demands behaviour incompatible with her values: <b>person–role</b> conflict (many whistle-blowing cases signal it).' },
    { q: 'Which of the following <b>lowers</b> group cohesiveness?', o: ['Competition with another group', 'A tough initiation process', 'Increasing group size', 'A recent success'], a: 2, why: 'Threat/competition, success and tough initiation raise cohesion; <b>diversity and larger size</b> tend to lower it (“Tight Squads Make Strong Teams”).' },
    { q: 'After two weeks, Priya cuts her own effort on a group report because she believes Jordan is doing almost nothing. Her behaviour is the:', o: ['Free rider effect', 'Sucker effect', 'Risky shift', 'Process gain'], a: 1, why: 'The <b>sucker effect</b> is lowering effort because you feel others are free riding, to restore equity. Jordan is the free rider.' },
    { q: 'Which of the following is NOT one of the textbook’s ways to counteract social loafing?', o: ['Make individual performance more visible', 'Increase performance feedback', 'Reward group performance', 'Make the group larger so effort is spread out'], a: 3, why: 'The text says to keep groups <b>small</b> so contributions are visible. The five counters: visible, interesting, indispensable, feedback, group rewards.' },
    { q: 'Cross-functional teams are most effective when, among other things, they have a <b>superordinate goal</b>. This is:', o: ['Any ambitious goal set by senior management', 'An outcome attainable only by collaboration, which overrides conflicting functional objectives', 'A goal that each specialty can reach alone', 'The team leader’s personal goal'], a: 1, why: 'A superordinate goal is attractive and can be achieved <b>only by collaboration</b>; it overrides conflicts such as finance versus design (Mustang: keep the name alive).' },
    { q: 'Which is a listed <b>challenge</b> of virtual teams?', o: ['Larger talent pool', 'Around-the-clock work', 'Trust', 'Reduced travel cost'], a: 2, why: 'Challenges: <b>trust, miscommunication, isolation, management issues</b>. The other three are the advantages.' },
    { q: 'Which one is NOT one of the four disadvantages of group decision making?', o: ['Time', 'Diffusion of responsibility', 'Domination', 'Groupthink'], a: 1, why: 'The disadvantages are <b>Time, Conflict, Domination, Groupthink</b>. Diffusion of responsibility is a (poor) <b>reason to use groups</b>.' },
    { q: 'A board approves a risky acquisition. A junior director who doubts the numbers stays silent, and the minutes record a “unanimous” decision. Which two groupthink symptoms are most directly shown?', o: ['Self-censorship and illusion of unanimity', 'Mindguards and illusion of morality', 'Stereotypes of outsiders and pressure for conformity', 'Illusion of invulnerability and rationalization'], a: 0, why: 'Talking herself out of dissent is <b>self-censorship</b>; silence read as agreement is the <b>illusion of unanimity</b>.' },
    { q: 'According to the textbook, the strongest cause of groupthink appears to be:', o: ['High cohesiveness', 'Promotion of a particular decision by the group leader', 'Large group size', 'Time pressure'], a: 1, why: 'Janis blamed cohesiveness, but the text says the <b>leader’s promotion of a decision</b> appears to be the strongest cause. Slide 27 leads with cohesiveness.' },
    { q: 'Members of a board who individually favour a high offer for a takeover target deliberate and end up agreeing on an even higher offer. This illustrates:', o: ['Groupthink', 'Diffusion of responsibility', 'Risky shift (group polarization)', 'Satisficing'], a: 2, why: 'Discussion <b>polarizes</b> the initial leaning. It can go either way: conservative starters become more conservative.' },
    { q: 'A company invites the public to submit and vote on new product designs, and uses the votes for final production decisions. This is:', o: ['Crowdsourcing', 'Consulting', 'A devil’s advocate approach', 'Overfitting'], a: 0, why: '<b>Crowdsourcing</b> outsources parts of a decision to a large crowd. It is not a formal consulting relationship.' }
  ],

  prompts: [
    { q: '<b>From last year’s Assignment #2 (Nov 2025, Dr. McAteer’s section), Q3:</b> You are a new manager at a medium-sized corporation who wants to manage groups effectively. (a) Explain the two models of group development discussed in 1BA3 and identify all their stages (3). (b) What do these two models tell you about managing groups? (1) (c) Explain social loafing and how it affects groups, and outline with examples two ways to overcome it (3). (d) Explain groupthink and describe, with examples, two reasons why it may occur (3).',
      name: '<b>Group development</b> (five-stage model and Gersick’s <b>punctuated equilibrium</b>), <b>social loafing</b> (free rider and sucker), and <b>groupthink</b> (Janis).',
      define: '<b>(a)</b> Model 1, the five stages: <b>forming</b> (members test the waters, ambiguity), <b>storming</b> (conflict, sorting out roles and responsibilities), <b>norming</b> (consensus, norms agreed, cohesion), <b>performing</b> (energy goes to the task), <b>adjourning</b> (groups with a definite lifespan or broken by layoffs disperse, with rites and rituals). Model 2, punctuated equilibrium (Gersick), for groups with a <b>deadline</b>: the <b>first meeting</b> (sets assumptions and precedents), <b>Phase 1</b> (inertia, little visible progress), the <b>midpoint transition</b> (at about halfway in time, a change of approach), <b>Phase 2</b> (the new approach plays out) and a <b>final burst</b> of activity. The textbook does not name Tuckman; just call it the five-stage model. <b>(c)</b> Social loafing is the tendency to withhold physical or intellectual effort on a group task; it is the motivation source of process loss. The <b>free rider effect</b> is lowering effort to ride on others; the <b>sucker effect</b> is lowering effort because you believe others are free riding. <b>(d)</b> Groupthink is the capacity for group pressure to damage the mental efficiency, reality testing and moral judgment of decision-making groups, so unanimity is stressed over decision quality (Janis; symptoms include illusion of invulnerability, rationalization, self-censorship, illusion of unanimity, mindguards).',
      apply: '<b>(b)</b> The five-stage model is a tool for <b>monitoring and troubleshooting</b> a new group: storming is normal and likely early, so do not panic. It applies mainly to new groups, and well-acquainted or highly structured groups can skip stages. Punctuated equilibrium says that for deadline groups the <b>first meeting</b> and the <b>midpoint</b> are the leverage points: do not expect radical progress in Phase 1, manage the midpoint carefully, and do not change the deadline. <b>(c)</b> Effect: lower group performance and resentment as sucker-effect behaviour spreads (one student free rides on a shared report; others cut back). <b>(d)</b> Two reasons it occurs (text and slide 27): <b>strong identification and high cohesiveness with concern for the group’s approval</b> (members self-censor to stay accepted), and <b>isolation from outside information</b> (NASA’s Hubble mirror team, led by a dominant leader, dismissed an outside firm and ignored three sets of danger signals). The text names the <b>leader promoting a particular decision</b> as the strongest cause, so a CEO who signals his preferred acquisition produces silent directors and a “unanimous” vote.',
      recommend: '<b>(b)</b> Prepare the first meeting, expect and manage storming (clarify roles and responsibilities early), set norms, and for deadline projects plan an explicit midpoint review. <b>(c)</b> Two fixes from the five: make individual performance <b>visible</b> (named sections, individual presentations, keep teams small) and give each member an <b>indispensable</b> input or add group rewards with feedback. <b>(d)</b> Leaders should avoid undue pressure for an outcome and focus on process, reward responsible dissent, bring in outside experts, and appoint a devil’s advocate.',
      also: 'Note on the question: it asks for “two models”. These are the five-stage model and punctuated equilibrium. Part (d)’s “two reasons” can also be answered with Janis’s original cause, high cohesiveness (Ch 7: cohesive groups induce conformity and pressure deviants). Related ideas: cohesiveness, conformity (Asch), process losses, group polarization.' },
    { q: 'A 14-person team at an outdoor-clothing firm is designing a winter jacket line in 12 weeks: designers in Toronto, engineers in Vancouver, marketing staff in Ho Chi Minh City, working mostly by email. The first meeting was a 20-minute video call with no agenda. By week 6 there is no agreed design; designers want a sleek fashion look, engineers want heavy waterproof materials. Two marketers have stopped contributing, a third says she will match their effort, Toronto staff distrust Vancouver’s cost estimates, nobody knows who makes the final call, and senior management keeps moving the launch date. <b>Diagnose the team’s problems and recommend a package of changes.</b>',
      name: '<b>Cross-functional and virtual team</b> problems, <b>punctuated equilibrium</b>, missing <b>shared mental models</b> and <b>superordinate goal</b>, <b>social loafing</b> (free rider / sucker), <b>role ambiguity</b>, and virtual-team <b>trust</b> and <b>miscommunication</b>.',
      define: 'A cross-functional team brings together different specialties to invent, design or deliver a product; a virtual team uses technology to collaborate across time, space and organizational boundaries. Punctuated equilibrium: deadline groups are shaped by the first meeting and a midpoint transition. Shared mental models: members share similar information about how to interact and what the task is. A superordinate goal is attainable only through collaboration. Social loafing is withholding effort in a group (free rider; sucker). Role ambiguity is a lack of clarity of job goals or methods, including limits of authority.',
      apply: 'The weak, agenda-free first meeting set poor assumptions for Phase 1. Week 6 is the midpoint of a 12-week deadline, the moment to change approach, and management’s deadline moves undermine it. The “technical” fight is a missing shared mental model, like the text’s “tough truck”, worsened by no superordinate goal. Two marketers show the free rider effect and the third the sucker effect; 14 members raise process losses. Distrust of Vancouver’s estimates is the virtual-team trust challenge, and lean email adds miscommunication. Nobody knowing who decides is role ambiguity plus missing rules and procedures.',
      recommend: 'Treat week 6 as the midpoint: a well-prepared synchronous session to evaluate Phase 1, clarify the brief with management, adopt a new approach and confirm Phase 2 resources, and ask management to fix the launch date. Set a superordinate goal, define “technical” together with samples, agree who has final authority, give the team autonomy, and pick a leader with strong people skills. Counter loafing (visible contributions, indispensable roles, feedback, group rewards, a smaller core). Apply virtual-team lessons: personalization, communication standards, cross-cultural training, weekly peer feedback, and Orange’s principles (small team, strong leadership, common working framework).',
      also: 'Hackman’s effectiveness criteria; psychological safety; information richness (Ch 10); the Name → Define → Apply → Recommend structure.' },
    { q: 'A warehouse night crew has worked together for years, puts new hires through a tough first month, and recently beat the day crew in a safety competition. When management raises the hourly picking target, the crew quietly agrees to keep the old pace; the one new member who picks faster is first lectured and then ignored. <b>Explain the crew’s behaviour using Chapter 7 concepts and advise management.</b>',
      name: '<b>Cohesiveness</b> (causes and consequences), <b>norms</b> and <b>conformity</b>, and a <b>performance norm</b> that conflicts with the organization’s.',
      define: 'Group cohesiveness is the degree to which a group is especially attractive to its members. Factors: threat and competition, success, member diversity, size and toughness of initiation. Consequences: more participation, more conformity, more success. Norms are collective expectations about each other’s behaviour; performance norms govern how much members work. In highly cohesive groups members’ productivity is similar, and whether it is high or low depends on whether the group accepts the organization’s norms.',
      apply: 'Cohesion comes from the tough first month (initiation), beating the day crew (competition and success), and probably stability and similarity. The consequence is conformity: the crew first increases communication to the deviant (the lecture) and then isolates him (ignores him). The crew rejects management’s target, so it is especially effective at limiting productivity, like informal output restrictions under piece rates.',
      recommend: 'Do not simply break up the crew: cohesion helps when goals align. Win acceptance of the target by explaining it and involving the crew in setting specific, challenging goals; reward group performance; improve the labour relations climate; and use the crew’s cohesion and competition (for example, against the day crew) as an asset.',
      also: 'Social loafing and group rewards; role of status; Hackman’s criteria; conformity (Ch 8).' },
    { q: 'A tight-knit executive team led by a forceful CEO approves a risky acquisition. The CEO made clear which way he leaned. A junior director who doubted the numbers stayed silent, telling herself everyone else must have checked them. The team dismissed an outside analyst’s warning as “people who don’t understand our business”, and one VP quietly kept unfavourable reports out of the meeting package. After the deal, several members said they “knew all along” it was a mistake. <b>Diagnose the group’s decision-making problems and recommend how the company should decide in future.</b>',
      name: '<b>Groupthink</b> (specific symptoms and causes), with <b>diffusion of responsibility</b> and possibly <b>polarization</b>, and the group-decision <b>disadvantages</b> (domination, conflict).',
      define: 'Groupthink is the capacity for group pressure to damage mental efficiency, reality testing and moral judgment so unanimity is stressed over quality (Janis). Diffusion of responsibility is members sharing the burden of negative consequences so no one is punished. Group polarization is discussion exaggerating members’ initial leaning. Devil’s advocate: a person appointed to challenge a plan’s weaknesses, objectively and unemotionally.',
      apply: 'Cause: the leader’s promotion of a particular decision (the strongest cause), plus identification with the group and a tight-knit team. Symptoms: <b>self-censorship</b> (the junior director), <b>illusion of unanimity</b> (silence read as agreement), <b>stereotypes of outsiders</b> and <b>rationalization</b> (the analyst dismissed), and <b>mindguards</b> (the VP withholding reports). The later “knew all along” remarks show biased hindsight, a feature of diffusion of responsibility; domination by the CEO also reduces the benefits of using a group.',
      recommend: 'The CEO should hold back from pushing a decision and focus on process; set norms that reward responsible dissent; bring in outside experts to challenge the plan; and appoint a devil’s advocate. Add the slide-28 voices (an idiosyncrasy-credit outlier, a disruptor, a safe route for whistle-blowing). Use evidence-based management (peer-reviewed and company data, not the boss’s preference). Build psychological safety. Note that polarization is a separate risk: a team already leaning toward a high bid will drift higher after discussion.',
      also: 'Cohesiveness and conformity (Ch 7, Ch 8); escalation of commitment (groups escalate more than individuals); crowdsourcing for outside views.' }
  ]
});
